      "because": "binds `params.api_key` of the `web_search` occupant, written `${SEARCH_API_KEY:-}`. Not required, because the cell filters the empty string and queries the endpoint anonymously -- that is the occupant's own declared contract (`contract.settings.api_key`), and requiring the key here would contradict the cell it configures. Set it when the configured endpoint wants a bearer token. It carries no value in this tree and never should: the token is the name of a secret, not the secret"
    }
  }
},
"sandbox_union": {
  "trust": "full",
  "network": "allow",
  "filesystem": "unrestricted",

  "because": "The widest occupant wins on every axis, and on every axis the widest is an occupant that declares no `params.sandbox` at all. Four do: `web_fetch` and `web_search` (egress IS what those two cells do -- `network: \"deny\"` would turn them off, and a process sandbox has no notion of an allowed host), and `file` and `edit` (they do their own I/O rather than starting a runner, so a process sandbox has nothing to bound here; their fence is `params.base_path`, checked lexically and again after canonicalisation). Any single one of those four therefore sets the whole hive to trust `full`, network `allow`, filesystem `unrestricted`. The five that DO carry a block -- `bash`, `unknown`, `build-draft`, `build-apply` and `schemas`, all of them `trust: restricted`, network `deny`, `filesystem.runtime: true`, the tightest in the library -- tighten nothing about the hive as a WHOLE: a union is not an average, and what they bound is what happens once dispatch has already chosen them. This reads uncomfortable and it is meant to: the radius already exists collectively and was invisible while it was spread over separate `config.json` files. Written down, it is what a replacement is measured against -- a single code-executing cell that needs exactly this is not a widening, and one that needs more is, visibly, in the diff of this file. Until 1.3.0 the sum also counted `mcp` and `vault`, two occupants no edge reached; they left the hive with GH #547, and a union over the occupants that actually answer is the narrower and the honest one."
},
"reentrancy": {
  "bash": {
    "reentrant": true,
    "because": "One-shot execution by ruling: no persistent session, no shell state and no working directory carried from one call to the next (a call that needs one writes `cd /x && cmd`). Two calls in flight are two processes that cannot observe each other, so a brain may fan a round out across this occupant. `params.max_concurrency: 4` caps how many run at once -- a queue, not a serialisation: the fifth call waits and still answers on its own `tool_result`."
  },
  "web_fetch": {
    "reentrant": true,
    "because": "A stateless request/response cell: one GET per call, nothing carried between calls, no cookie jar and no session. `params.max_concurrency: 4` caps the parallel fetches for the endpoint's sake, which delays a call but never reorders or merges one."
  },
  "web_search": {
    "reentrant": true,
    "because": "A stateless request/response cell against the configured endpoint: one query per call, no cursor and no pagination state to be confused by a second call. `params.max_concurrency: 8`, same reading as above."
  },
  "unknown": {
    "reentrant": true,
    "because": "It formats a string out of the hop it was handed and emits it. It reads nothing, writes nothing and executes nothing, so there is no state for a concurrent call to reach. Declared explicitly although it is not a tool: #286's hazard is a swap that quietly turns a parallel tool round sequential, and a declaration with a hole cannot catch that -- the occupant nobody declared is the one whose serialisation surprises the caller."
  },
  "build-draft": {
    "reentrant": true,
    "because": "It reads a hop and a turn and writes a turn: no disk, no network, no state of its own. The correlation of a round runs over `tool_call_id`, which travels in the CONTEXT of that round's own message, so two builds in flight cannot reach each other's. What they DO share is the builder at the OS level -- one baumeister for the whole colony -- and that is a queue there, not a serialisation here: the second round waits for its own answer and still gets it under its own id."
  },
  "build-apply": {
    "reentrant": true,

      "because": "The same shape: it carries a manifest out and a receipt back, keeping nothing. Two submissions in flight are two manifests the colony rolls off one after the other, in the order they reach the mutation door -- and because a manifest has no rollback, that order is the colony's to decide and never this cell's to assume."
    },
    "file": {
      "reentrant": true,
      "because": "A stateless request/response cell over the filesystem: one op per call, no handle and no cursor carried between calls, so `params.max_concurrency` is a queue and never a serialisation. Two READS of one file cannot disturb each other; two WRITES of one path race in the filesystem rather than in this cell, and that is the caller's ordering to make -- which is exactly why the neighbour `edit` answers this question the other way."
    },
    "edit": {
      "reentrant": false,
      "because": "The one `false` in this block, and it is the reason the block exists. An edit is a read-modify-write with no lock and no tempfile+rename (`cell-types.md` \u00a7 edit: \"Concurrent edit on the same file: race condition possible ... The caller topology serializes if needed\"). A caller cannot see that hazard from outside, so this occupant serialises instead of asking it to: `params.max_concurrency: 1`, a strict queue. A brain may still fan a round out across the other occupants; the edits of that round run one after another."
    },
    "schemas": {
      "reentrant": true,
      "because": "It reads a list of names and writes a list of declarations out of a table that is compiled into its own script. No disk, no network, no state, nothing shared -- two start-up questions in flight cannot reach each other, and they are the only kind of message it ever gets."
    }
  },
